WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.766
Windows, Linux, and macOS can all support local inference.

00:00:04.766 --> 00:00:14.980
The practical choice depends on hardware support, operator skill, application needs, security controls, automation, and the ability to maintain the environment.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.746
NVIDIA acceleration commonly relies on CUDA-compatible drivers and runtimes.

00:00:19.746 --> 00:00:27.236
AMD support may use ROCm or other compatible paths depending on hardware and operating system.

00:00:27.236 --> 00:00:32.456
Vulkan can provide a cross-platform acceleration option for supported runtimes.

00:00:32.456 --> 00:00:40.853
Apple Silicon uses Metal and unified memory, allowing CPU and GPU components to share a memory pool.

00:00:40.853 --> 00:00:46.074
Available capacity, bandwidth, thermals, and model support still set limits.

00:00:46.074 --> 00:00:50.840
Unified memory should not be treated as unlimited VRAM.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:58.330
Driver and runtime versions must align with the hardware, operating system, inference software, and libraries.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:08.089
A powerful GPU can perform poorly or fail to load a model when the acceleration stack is unsupported or inconsistent.

00:01:08.089 --> 00:01:11.947
Stability matters more than chasing every update.

00:01:11.947 --> 00:01:19.891
Maintain a known working configuration, record versions, review release changes, test upgrades, and preserve rollback options.

00:01:19.891 --> 00:01:24.657
Security updates and unsupported dependencies still need timely attention.

00:01:24.657 --> 00:01:28.970
The software environment is part of capacity planning.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:38.729
Background applications, display usage, antivirus or security scanning, containers, virtual machines, and multiple runtimes can consume memory and complicate troubleshooting.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:40.772
Standardize before tuning.
